{"meta": {"chapter": "02", "section": "02", "title": "Work and Energy", "course": "dynamics", "learning_objectives": ["Calculate work done by forces", "Apply the work-energy theorem", "Distinguish conservative and non-conservative forces", "Use energy conservation to solve problems"], "concept_ids": ["work", "kinetic_energy", "potential_energy", "conservation_of_energy"], "physics_prereqs": ["newtons_second_law"], "math_prereqs": ["integrals"], "units_focus": ["J", "N", "m"], "common_errors": ["sign", "reference_point", "conservative_nonconservative"], "estimated_time_minutes": 55, "prereq_section_ids": ["dynamics/02/01"], "practice_ids": ["work_zero_concept", "kinetic_energy_numeric", "friction_work_numeric", "work_energy_speed", "incline_energy_steps", "conservative_force_concept"]}, "content": "# Work and Energy\n\n> **\"Energy methods compress long force histories into one clean before-and-after statement.\"**\n\n---\n\n## Prerequisites\n\n`newtons_second_law` `integrals`\n\n---\n\n## Learning Objectives\n\n1. **Calculate** work done by constant and variable forces\n2. **Apply** the work-energy theorem to particle motion\n3. **Distinguish** conservative and non-conservative forces\n4. **Use** energy accounting when force-by-force acceleration analysis is cumbersome\n\n---\n\n## Opening: The Crumple Zone\n\nWhen a car hits a barrier at 25 m/s, the driver survives not because the crash is gentle but because the front of the car is engineered to do a very specific amount of work. A modern crumple zone deliberately deforms, spreading the change in the car's kinetic energy over roughly half a meter of crush distance instead of a few millimeters of rigid contact. Because force times distance must equal the kinetic energy removed, lengthening the distance lowers the peak force on the occupant. A rigid bumper would stop the same car in a centimeter and multiply the deceleration force by a hundred.\n\nThat is the entire philosophy of energy methods in one example. Instead of tracking the violent, millisecond-by-millisecond force history of the collision, an engineer can write a single statement: the kinetic energy at impact equals the work absorbed by the structure. The same idea sizes elevator buffers, ski-lift catch springs, regenerative brakes, and pile drivers.\n\n> **The Big Picture:** Newton's second law answers \"what is happening right now.\" Work and energy answer \"what changed between here and there.\" For many design questions, the second is the cheaper and clearer question.\n\n---\n\n## Why Engineers Switch to Energy\n\nNewton's second law is local. It tells you what acceleration is happening at an instant. Energy methods are cumulative. They tell you how the system state changes between two positions.\n\nThat is powerful when:\n\n- the motion path is known but time is not important\n- several forces act over a distance\n- you care about final speed more than intermediate acceleration\n- springs, gravity, and friction all interact in one system\n\nIn those cases, work and energy often reduce a multi-step force analysis to one well-structured equation.\n\n## Work Done by a Force\n\n$$\n\\boxed{W = \\int \\vec{F} \\cdot d\\vec{r}}\n$$\n\nFor a constant force acting at angle $\\theta$ to the displacement:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{W = Fd\\cos \\theta}\n$$\n\nThe dot product is the key idea: only the component of force along the displacement does work. A force perpendicular to motion (such as the normal force on a sliding block, or the tension in a string holding a ball in circular motion) does zero work no matter how large it is.\n\n<inline-check question=\"A 50 N force is applied at 60 degrees above a horizontal floor while a crate slides 4 m horizontally. How much work does the force do on the crate?\" explanation=\"Only the horizontal component does work: W = F*d*cos(theta) = 50 * 4 * cos(60) = 50 * 4 * 0.5 = 100 J. The vertical component of the push is perpendicular to the horizontal displacement and contributes nothing.\" options='[{\"text\":\"100 J\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"200 J\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"173 J\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\nThe sign of work matters:\n\n- positive work adds kinetic energy\n- negative work removes kinetic energy\n- zero work means the force does not change the body's speed through that displacement\n\n## Common Work Models\n\n| Force type | Useful expression | Sign intuition |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Constant force | $W = Fd\\cos \\theta$ | Positive if force helps motion |\n| Gravity | $W_g = -mg\\Delta h$ | Negative when moving upward |\n| Spring | $W_s = \\frac{1}{2}k(x_1^2 - x_2^2)$ | Depends on compression change |\n| Kinetic friction | $W_f = -f_k d = -\\mu_k N d$ | Always resists sliding |\n\nThe spring expression deserves a note, because students often misremember its sign. The force a linear spring exerts is $F = -kx$, opposing the stretch. Integrating that force from $x_1$ to $x_2$,\n\n$$\nW_s = \\int_{x_1}^{x_2} (-kx)\\, dx = \\tfrac{1}{2}k\\left(x_1^2 - x_2^2\\right).\n$$\n\nSo a spring that relaxes from a compression of $x_1$ to its free length ($x_2 = 0$) does positive work $\\tfrac{1}{2}kx_1^2$ on whatever it pushes, which is exactly the energy it stored while being compressed.\n\n## Kinetic Energy\n\n$$\n\\boxed{T = \\frac{1}{2}mv^2}\n$$\n\nKinetic energy depends on speed, not direction. A car moving east at 20 m/s and one moving west at 20 m/s have the same kinetic energy.\n\n## The Work-Energy Theorem\n\n$$\n\\boxed{W_{net} = \\Delta T = T_2 - T_1}\n$$\n\nExpanded for a particle:\n\n$$\nW_{net} = \\frac{1}{2}mv_2^2 - \\frac{1}{2}mv_1^2\n$$\n\n### Where the Theorem Comes From\n\nThe theorem is not a separate law; it is Newton's second law integrated over distance. Start from $\\sum \\vec{F} = m\\vec{a}$ along the path and take the dot product with $d\\vec{r}$:\n\n$$\n\\sum \\vec{F} \\cdot d\\vec{r} = m\\,\\vec{a} \\cdot d\\vec{r}.\n$$\n\nUsing $\\vec{a} = \\dfrac{d\\vec{v}}{dt}$ and $d\\vec{r} = \\vec{v}\\,dt$, the right side becomes $m\\,\\vec{v} \\cdot d\\vec{v}$. Integrating from state 1 to state 2,\n\n$$\n\\int_1^2 \\sum \\vec{F} \\cdot d\\vec{r} = m \\int_1^2 \\vec{v} \\cdot d\\vec{v} = \\tfrac{1}{2}mv_2^2 - \\tfrac{1}{2}mv_1^2.\n$$\n\nThe left side is exactly the net work, so $W_{net} = T_2 - T_1$. This derivation explains why the theorem is so general: it inherits the validity of Newton's second law and adds no new assumptions.\n\nThis is the energy version of cause and effect:\n\n- forces do work\n- net work changes kinetic energy\n- changed kinetic energy changes speed\n\n<dynamic-scenario\n  topic=\"Battery Range on an Electric Scooter\"\n  context=\"A mobility engineer is comparing two electric scooters on the same downhill route. One has lower rolling resistance, while the other has regenerative braking. The engineer wants to know how much of the route's potential energy becomes wheel speed and how much is dissipated or recovered.\"\n  course=\"dynamics\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Conservative and Non-Conservative Forces\n\n### Conservative forces\n\nThese are forces whose work depends only on the start and end states, not the exact path.\n\nExamples:\n\n- gravity\n- ideal spring force\n\nFor these forces, we introduce potential energy $V$ and write:\n\n$$\nW_c = -\\Delta V\n$$\n\n### Non-conservative forces\n\nThese depend on the actual path or on energy dissipation mechanisms.\n\nExamples:\n\n- kinetic friction\n- drag\n- active propulsion losses\n\nFor these, it is often clearer to keep them as explicit work terms such as $W_{nc}$.\n\n## Two Useful Energy Forms\n\nWhen only conservative forces act:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{T_1 + V_1 = T_2 + V_2}\n$$\n\nWhen non-conservative work is present:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{T_1 + V_1 + W_{nc} = T_2 + V_2}\n$$\n\nThis second equation is usually the safer default in engineering problems because real systems often include friction, damping, or external actuators.\n\n<parameter-explorer config='{\"title\":\"Spring-Launched Slider Across a Rough Patch: v = sqrt((k*x^2 - 2*mu_k*m*g*d)/m)\",\"sliders\":[{\"name\":\"k\",\"label\":\"Spring stiffness\",\"min\":100,\"max\":2000,\"step\":50,\"unit\":\"N/m\",\"default\":800},{\"name\":\"x\",\"label\":\"Spring compression\",\"min\":0.02,\"max\":0.4,\"step\":0.01,\"unit\":\"m\",\"default\":0.15},{\"name\":\"m\",\"label\":\"Slider mass\",\"min\":0.2,\"max\":3,\"step\":0.1,\"unit\":\"kg\",\"default\":0.5},{\"name\":\"mu_k\",\"label\":\"Friction on rough patch\",\"min\":0,\"max\":0.6,\"step\":0.05,\"unit\":\"\",\"default\":0.25},{\"name\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Rough patch length\",\"min\":0,\"max\":1.5,\"step\":0.05,\"unit\":\"m\",\"default\":0.6}],\"outputs\":[{\"label\":\"Spring work stored/released\",\"unit\":\"J\",\"expr\":\"0.5*k*x^2\"},{\"label\":\"Friction energy lost\",\"unit\":\"J\",\"expr\":\"mu_k*m*9.81*d\"},{\"label\":\"Final speed at sensor\",\"unit\":\"m/s\",\"expr\":\"sqrt((k*x^2 - 2*mu_k*m*9.81*d)/m)\"}],\"plot\":{\"x\":\"x\",\"output\":2}}'></parameter-explorer>\n\nWatch the final speed collapse to zero (and the expression go imaginary) when the friction energy lost exceeds the spring work stored: the slider would stall on the rough patch before reaching the sensor.\n\n## Assumption Callout: What Energy Methods Hide\n\nEnergy methods are powerful because they compress motion into a before-and-after statement. That efficiency also means they often hide the detailed time history.\n\nWhen you use work and energy, you are usually assuming that:\n\n- the start and end states are the main quantities of interest,\n- the chosen work terms capture the dominant energy transfers,\n- and neglected losses are small enough not to change the design decision materially.\n\nThat is why energy methods are excellent for speed, height, compression, and stopping-distance estimates, but less informative when you care about peak force, transient acceleration, or exact timing.\n\n## Worked Examples\n\n### Example 2.1: Spring-Launched Slider with Friction\n\n**Problem:** A 0.5 kg slider rests against a spring of stiffness $k = 800$ N/m on a horizontal track. The spring is compressed 0.15 m and released. After leaving the spring, the slider crosses a rough patch 0.6 m long with $\\mu_k = 0.25$ before reaching a sensor. Find (a) the launch speed as the slider leaves the spring, and (b) the speed at the sensor.\n\n**Free body diagram (rough patch).** Picture the slider on the horizontal track: weight $W = mg$ down, normal force $N$ up, and kinetic friction $f_k$ pointing backward against the motion. There is no vertical motion, so $N = mg$.\n\n**Step 1: Work done by the spring.** The spring relaxes from $x_1 = 0.15$ m to $x_2 = 0$:\n\n$$\nW_s = \\tfrac{1}{2}k\\left(x_1^2 - x_2^2\\right) = \\tfrac{1}{2}(800)(0.15^2 - 0) = 9.00 \\text{ J}\n$$\n\n**Step 2: Launch speed (part a).** On the smooth section the only horizontal work is the spring's, and the slider starts from rest, so $W_s = \\tfrac{1}{2}mv_1^2$:\n\n$$\nv_1 = \\sqrt{\\frac{2W_s}{m}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{2(9.00)}{0.5}} = \\boxed{6.00 \\text{ m/s}}\n$$\n\n**Step 3: Friction work on the rough patch.** First the normal force, then friction:\n\n$$\nN = mg = (0.5)(9.81) = 4.905 \\text{ N}\n$$\n\n$$\nf_k = \\mu_k N = (0.25)(4.905) = 1.226 \\text{ N}\n$$\n\n$$\nW_f = -f_k d = -(1.226)(0.6) = -0.736 \\text{ J}\n$$\n\n**Step 4: Speed at the sensor (part b).** Apply the work-energy theorem across the whole motion, from rest to the sensor:\n\n$$\nW_s + W_f = \\tfrac{1}{2}mv_2^2\n$$\n\n$$\n9.00 - 0.736 = \\tfrac{1}{2}(0.5)v_2^2 = 0.25\\,v_2^2\n$$\n\n$$\nv_2 = \\sqrt{\\frac{8.264}{0.25}} = \\boxed{5.75 \\text{ m/s}}\n$$\n\nThe slider arrives a little slower than its launch speed because the rough patch removed 0.74 J of the 9.00 J the spring delivered.\n\n---\n\n### Example 2.2: Block Sliding Down an Incline with Friction\n\n**Problem:** A 5 kg block slides 3 m down a $30^\\circ$ incline with $\\mu_k = 0.2$. Starting from rest, find the final speed.\n\n**Free body diagram (on the incline).** Tilt the axes with the slope: weight $mg$ down, normal force $N$ perpendicular to the surface, and friction $f_k$ up the slope opposing the downhill slide. The component of weight along the slope drives the motion; the component perpendicular to the slope sets the normal force.\n\n### Step A: Define the initial and final states\n\n- Initial kinetic energy: $T_1 = 0$ because the block starts from rest\n- Final potential energy can be taken as zero at the bottom\n- Initial height change:\n\n$$\nh = 3\\sin 30^\\circ = 1.5 \\text{ m}\n$$\n\n### Step B: Compute the gravitational potential drop\n\n$$\nV_1 - V_2 = mgh = 5(9.81)(1.5) = 73.6 \\text{ J}\n$$\n\n### Step C: Compute friction work\n\nFirst find the normal force:\n\n$$\nN = mg\\cos 30^\\circ = 42.5 \\text{ N}\n$$\n\nThen the friction force:\n\n$$\nf_k = \\mu_k N = 0.2(42.5) = 8.50 \\text{ N}\n$$\n\nWork of friction:\n\n$$\nW_f = -f_k d = -(8.50)(3) = -25.5 \\text{ J}\n$$\n\n### Step D: Write the energy equation\n\n$$\nT_1 + V_1 + W_f = T_2 + V_2\n$$\n\n$$\n0 + 73.6 - 25.5 = \\frac{1}{2}(5)v_2^2 + 0\n$$\n\n$$\n48.1 = 2.5v_2^2\n$$\n\n$$\nv_2 = \\sqrt{19.24} = \\boxed{4.39 \\text{ m/s}}\n$$\n\nThe speed is lower than the frictionless case (which would give $v_2 = \\sqrt{2gh} = 5.42$ m/s) because some of the gravitational energy was dissipated by friction.\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Build the Energy Balance for the Sliding Block\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"The 5 kg block slides 3 m down a 30 degree incline. What vertical height does it drop?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"h = 3 m (the path length)\",\"h = 3*sin(30) = 1.5 m\",\"h = 3*cos(30) = 2.6 m\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Potential energy depends on the vertical drop, not the path length: h = d*sin(theta) = 3*sin(30) = 1.5 m. Using the full 3 m is the most common energy-method error.\"},{\"prompt\":\"How much gravitational potential energy is released over that drop? (g = 9.81)\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"mgh = 5*9.81*1.5 = 73.6 J\",\"mgd = 5*9.81*3 = 147 J\",\"mgh = 5*9.81*1.5*cos(30) = 63.7 J\"],\"answerIndex\":0,\"reveal\":\"V1 - V2 = mgh = 5*9.81*1.5 = 73.6 J. This is the energy available to become kinetic energy minus friction losses.\"},{\"prompt\":\"Friction is f_k = mu_k*mg*cos(30) = 8.50 N over 3 m. What is the friction work?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"W_f = +25.5 J\",\"W_f = -25.5 J\",\"W_f = -73.6 J\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Friction opposes the slide, so its work is negative: W_f = -f_k*d = -8.50*3 = -25.5 J. Forgetting this minus sign is the second classic mistake.\"},{\"reveal\":\"Closing the balance: T1 + V1 + W_f = T2 + V2 gives 0 + 73.6 - 25.5 = 0.5*5*v^2, so v = sqrt(19.24) = 4.39 m/s. Note we never needed the acceleration or the time, only the start and end states.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\n---\n\n### Example 2.3: Stopping Distance of a Braking Car\n\n**Problem:** A 1200 kg car travels at 25 m/s when the driver locks the brakes on dry pavement, $\\mu_k = 0.7$. Using energy methods, find the stopping distance.\n\n**Free body diagram.** On level ground: weight $mg$ down, normal force $N = mg$ up, and friction $f_k$ backward, opposing motion. Friction is the only force doing work along the road.\n\n**Step 1: Initial kinetic energy.**\n\n$$\nT_1 = \\tfrac{1}{2}mv_0^2 = \\tfrac{1}{2}(1200)(25^2) = 375{,}000 \\text{ J}\n$$\n\n**Step 2: Friction force.**\n\n$$\nf_k = \\mu_k mg = (0.7)(1200)(9.81) = 8240 \\text{ N}\n$$\n\n**Step 3: Work-energy balance.** The car ends at rest, so all kinetic energy is removed by friction over distance $d$:\n\n$$\n-f_k d = 0 - T_1 \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad d = \\frac{T_1}{f_k} = \\frac{375{,}000}{8240} = \\boxed{45.5 \\text{ m}}\n$$\n\nNotice the design lesson hidden in the algebra: $d = \\dfrac{v_0^2}{2\\mu_k g}$ scales with the **square** of speed. Doubling the speed quadruples the stopping distance, which is exactly why posted speed limits dominate crash severity.\n\n## What Students Usually Miss\n\n- They forget that friction work is negative.\n- They mix up height change and path length.\n- They use $mgh$ with the full incline length instead of the vertical drop.\n- They assume \"energy is conserved\" even when friction is present, rather than writing the more general balance with $W_{nc}$.\n\n## Engineering Trade-Off: Detailed Force History Versus State-to-State Clarity\n\nWork-energy methods often outperform force-balance methods when the path is known and the final state matters most. But that convenience comes with a modeling trade-off.\n\nEnergy analysis is strong when you need:\n\n- final speed,\n- required spring compression,\n- climb or descent capability,\n- or total dissipation over a travel distance.\n\nIt is weaker when you need:\n\n- peak actuator load,\n- contact force history,\n- instantaneous acceleration,\n- or control-oriented timing.\n\nIn practice, engineers often use both views together: energy to check feasibility quickly, and force-time analysis to refine the loads that control design.\n\n## Comparative Example: Same Ramp, Two Design Choices\n\nSuppose two 12 kg delivery carts descend the same 4 m ramp segment with a 1.2 m vertical drop.\n\n- **Cart A:** conventional wheels, effective non-conservative loss of 18 J\n- **Cart B:** improved bearings and lower rolling resistance, effective non-conservative loss of 8 J\n\nUsing the energy balance,\n\n$$\nT_1 + V_1 + W_{nc} = T_2 + V_2\n$$\n\nand assuming both start from rest:\n\n$$\nmgh + W_{nc} = \\frac{1}{2}mv^2\n$$\n\n### Cart A\n\n$$\n(12)(9.81)(1.2) - 18 = \\frac{1}{2}(12)v_A^2\n$$\n\n$$\n141.3 - 18 = 6v_A^2\n$$\n\n$$\nv_A = 4.53 \\text{ m/s}\n$$\n\n### Cart B\n\n$$\n(12)(9.81)(1.2) - 8 = 6v_B^2\n$$\n\n$$\nv_B = 4.71 \\text{ m/s}\n$$\n\nThe difference is small in equation count but meaningful in design interpretation. Lower dissipation improves speed efficiency, but it may also increase stopping demand downstream. Energy methods make that trade-off visible very quickly.\n\n## Engineering Applications\n\n| Application | How work and energy are used |\n| --- | --- |\n| Vehicle crumple zones | Crush distance is sized so absorbed work limits peak deceleration force |\n| Regenerative braking | Kinetic energy is captured as stored charge instead of friction heat |\n| Elevator buffer springs | Spring work $\\tfrac{1}{2}kx^2$ must absorb the car's kinetic energy on overtravel |\n| Pile drivers and forging | Dropped-mass potential energy converts to plastic work in the workpiece |\n| Roller coasters | Energy balance sets minimum hill clearance and loop entry speed |\n\n> **Engineering in Action:** Whether the device stores energy (a launch spring), dissipates it (a brake), or recovers it (a regenerative drive), the design question is the same accounting statement: where does the kinetic energy go, and over what distance.\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\n<interactive-quiz\n  question=\"A force acts perpendicular to the displacement of a particle. What is the work done by that force?\"\n  conceptId=\"work_energy\"\n  options='[{\"text\":\"Positive because the force is nonzero.\",\"isCorrect\":false},{\"text\":\"Negative because the angle is 90 degrees.\",\"isCorrect\":false},{\"text\":\"Zero because the dot product is zero.\",\"isCorrect\":true},{\"text\":\"It cannot be determined without the mass.\",\"isCorrect\":false}]'\n  explanation=\"Correct. Work depends on the component of force along the displacement. A force perpendicular to motion contributes no work.\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n<interactive-quiz\n  question=\"Why is friction treated as negative work in a sliding problem?\"\n  conceptId=\"conservation_of_energy\"\n  options='[{\"text\":\"Because friction always points downward.\",\"isCorrect\":false},{\"text\":\"Because friction removes mechanical energy from the moving body over the displacement.\",\"isCorrect\":true},{\"text\":\"Because friction has no units.\",\"isCorrect\":false}]'\n  explanation=\"Exactly. Kinetic friction acts opposite the relative motion, so its dot product with displacement is negative and it reduces the body's mechanical energy.\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Summary\n\n### Key Equations\n\n| Concept | Equation |\n| --- | --- |\n| Work (general) | $W = \\int \\vec{F} \\cdot d\\vec{r}$ |\n| Work (constant force) | $W = Fd\\cos\\theta$ |\n| Kinetic energy | $T = \\tfrac{1}{2}mv^2$ |\n| Work-energy theorem | $W_{net} = T_2 - T_1$ |\n| Spring work | $W_s = \\tfrac{1}{2}k(x_1^2 - x_2^2)$ |\n| Gravity work | $W_g = -mg\\Delta h$ |\n| Friction work | $W_f = -\\mu_k N d$ |\n| Energy balance with losses | $T_1 + V_1 + W_{nc} = T_2 + V_2$ |\n\n### Key Takeaways\n\n- Work is the energy transfer caused by a force acting through a displacement.\n- Net work changes kinetic energy, and the theorem is just $\\sum F = ma$ integrated over distance.\n- Conservative forces can be converted into potential-energy terms.\n- Friction and other dissipative effects should remain explicit unless you are modeling a truly conservative system.\n- Stopping distance scales with the square of speed, a direct consequence of the energy balance.\n\n## Concept Map\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"work\",\"kinetic_energy\",\"potential_energy\",\"conservation_of_energy\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## What's Next?\n\nIn **Section 2.3: Impulse and Momentum**, we move from force-through-distance to force-through-time and study collisions, impacts, and short-duration loads.\n", "simulation": null, "illustration": null, "practice": {"problems": [{"id": "work_zero_concept", "type": "multiple_choice", "stem": "A force acts exactly perpendicular to the displacement of a particle. How much work does that force do?", "options": ["Positive work", "Negative work", "Zero work", "The work cannot be found without mass"], "correct_index": 2, "explanation": "Work depends on the force component along the displacement. A perpendicular force has zero dot product with the displacement and therefore does zero work.", "difficulty": "easy", "concept_ids": ["work"], "misconception_id": "perpendicular_force_work"}, {"id": "kinetic_energy_numeric", "type": "numeric", "stem": "A 6 kg cart moves at 4 m/s. Find its kinetic energy.", "final_answer": {"value": 48, "unit": "J", "tolerance": 0.1}, "difficulty": "easy", "concept_ids": ["kinetic_energy"]}, {"id": "friction_work_numeric", "type": "numeric", "stem": "A 10 N kinetic-friction force acts over 5 m opposite the motion. Find the work done by friction.", "final_answer": {"value": -50, "unit": "J", "tolerance": 0.1}, "difficulty": "medium", "concept_ids": ["work"]}, {"id": "work_energy_speed", "type": "numeric", "stem": "A 3 kg particle starts from rest and receives 24 J of net work. Find its final speed.", "final_answer": {"value": 4, "unit": "m/s", "tolerance": 0.1}, "difficulty": "medium", "concept_ids": ["work", "kinetic_energy"]}, {"id": "incline_energy_steps", "type": "step_based", "stem": "A 4 kg block slides down a rough incline and drops 2 m vertically while friction does -12 J of work. Starting from rest, find the final speed.", "steps": [{"step_id": 1, "description": "Compute the gravitational potential-energy drop mgh.", "expected_value": 78.48, "unit": "J", "tolerance": 0.2}, {"step_id": 2, "description": "Add the non-conservative work to get the final kinetic energy.", "expected_value": 66.48, "unit": "J", "tolerance": 0.2}, {"step_id": 3, "description": "Solve 1/2 m v^2 = 66.48 for v.", "expected_value": 5.76, "unit": "m/s", "tolerance": 0.1}], "final_answer": {"value": 5.76, "unit": "m/s", "tolerance": 0.1}, "difficulty": "hard", "concept_ids": ["conservation_of_energy", "potential_energy", "kinetic_energy", "work"]}, {"id": "conservative_force_concept", "type": "multiple_choice", "stem": "Which force below is conservative in the idealized mechanical model used in this course?", "options": ["Kinetic friction", "Dry drag", "Gravity", "Rolling resistance"], "correct_index": 2, "explanation": "Gravity is conservative because its work depends only on the change in position, not on the path taken between the two states.", "difficulty": "medium", "concept_ids": ["potential_energy", "conservation_of_energy"]}]}, "misconceptions": [{"id": "friction_positive_work", "pattern": "work_sign_error", "description": "Treating friction work as positive in a sliding problem", "trigger": "learner adds friction work to the energy budget when friction opposes the displacement", "feedback": "Use the dot product idea. If friction points opposite the displacement, it removes mechanical energy and its work is negative.", "rail_action": "explain"}, {"id": "height_vs_path", "pattern": "geometry_error", "description": "Using the path length instead of the vertical height change in gravitational potential energy", "trigger": "learner substitutes the incline length directly into mgh", "feedback": "Gravitational potential energy depends on vertical height change, not on the distance traveled along the path. Find the vertical drop first, then use mgh.", "rail_action": "represent"}, {"id": "energy_conserved_with_friction", "pattern": "model_selection_error", "description": "Using pure energy conservation even when non-conservative work is present", "trigger": "learner writes T1 + V1 = T2 + V2 despite friction or drag in the problem", "feedback": "When dissipative forces are present, keep them as explicit work terms. The safer equation is T1 + V1 + W_nc = T2 + V2.", "rail_action": "practice"}, {"id": "perpendicular_force_work", "pattern": "concept_error", "description": "Assuming any nonzero force must do nonzero work", "trigger": "learner assigns work to a force perpendicular to the displacement", "feedback": "Only the force component along the displacement does work. A force perpendicular to the motion does zero work because the dot product is zero.", "rail_action": "explain"}]}